  "title": "pH of Strong Acids and Bases",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "cxqoxlvwciopnj9",
                "text": "The Relative Strengths of Strong Acids and Bases"
              }
            ],
            "id": "w1278rs5up8bqef",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Strong acids, such as hydrochloric acid (HCl), hydrogen bromide (HBr), and hydrogen iodide (HI), all exhibit the same strength in water. The water molecule is such a strong base compared to the conjugate bases Cl"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", Br"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and I"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " that ionization of these strong acids is essentially complete in aqueous solutions. In solvents less basic than water, we find HCl, HBr, and HI differ markedly in their tendency to give up a proton to the solvent. For example, when dissolved in ethanol (a weaker base than water), the extent of ionization increases in the order HCl < HBr < HI, so HI is demonstrated to be the strongest of these acids. The inability to discern differences in strength among strong acids dissolved in water is known as the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "leveling effect"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The observation that acid–base strength is limited to that of the solvent’s characteristic acid and base species. In water, all strong acids have the same strength and are completely converted into solutions of H3O"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " ions, and all strong bases are similarly completely converted into solutions of OH"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "−"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " ions."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "609071648",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3956928456",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of water."
              }
            ],
            "id": "8ku83mwv7ohr93y",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Water also exerts a leveling effect on the strengths of strong bases. For example, the oxide ion, O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the amide ion, NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", are such strong bases that they react completely with water:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "7rw9tavur8cidgq",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "f8ytquviex1siub",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dbikvp0johui4im",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm O^{2-}(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\\\{\\mathrm{NH}}_2^-(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm{NH}}_3(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)\\end{array}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Therefore, O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2−"
              },
              {
                "text": " and NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " appear to have the same base strength in water; they both give a 100% yield of hydroxide ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "8yzholmzn71nka9",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The number of strong acids and bases is relatively small. It is a good idea to memorize the following list. In this course, you can assume that other acids and bases you may encounter are weak."
              }
            ],
            "id": "vu4g7tg3mno9ogw",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "strong": true,
                "text": "Common Strong Acids and Bases"
              }
            ],
            "id": "sx9e28ro5y6ympr",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "5619lv7cumzfzq0",
                        "text": "Six Strong Acids"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "dhplglj0ztgqm7g",
                    "type": "th"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "1kxqmjpwzvw9bo4",
                        "text": "Six Strong Bases"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "pb8792077w5t0i5",
                    "type": "th"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "n0ym7vnrr0hep3s",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "vki3eqfk23sdhq8",
                        "text": "HClO"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "0oik88vypchz6vx",
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "4"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "1vsklqs6pczfbw8",
                        "text": "      perchloric acid"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "ru1fy1lhyx8aftg",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "u5l6qzv2kh48fhy",
                        "text": "LiOH      lithium hydroxide"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "onj9g8zc59ubd6o",
                    "type": "td"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "d7puxs14c4c7mjc",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "4ewz85v34f4vjqv",
                        "text": "HCl     hydrochloric acid"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "5hqerosy37nai0g",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "xo22n8hna3so4g8",
                        "text": "NaOH     sodium hydroxide"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "626439djtzac4s0",
                    "type": "td"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "qe8t9istzr2rk1k",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "red58rxbtbe3ixh",
                        "text": "HBr     hydrobromic acid"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "jphbln8qur4n4cp",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "rhjincpeurpvu2z",
                        "text": "KOH     potassium hydroxide"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "28yrquwthxi9udm",
                    "type": "td"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "pw6n3ra8sxbwukb",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "qe1c7azu440nvos",
                        "text": "HI     hydroiodic acid"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1pxajxs0osnxccl",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "irk6iq8znprx2up",
                        "text": "Ca(OH)"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "7yrjpjj1wcy5ve2",
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "2"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "r6wvah4ne6fy79x",
                        "text": "      calcium hydroxide"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "93db1mf6l8ufskt",
                    "type": "td"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4iv2qd6lzrwz0o4",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "spjc58of0bhlqen",
                        "text": "HNO"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "pn5dq71rdwsvvlk",
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "3"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "fowk7eugxcu737y",
                        "text": "     nitric acid"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "0k6z3iihabpeqzg",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "omeqt3gkkwnwmtt",
                        "text": "Sr(OH)"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "spm4gfvp97e0e9p",
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "2"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "ykqclq54kj5itlc",
                        "text": "     strontium hydroxide"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "z8fil2j4yqscxfw",
                    "type": "td"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "etzntwqcixdrtrw",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "4n12gss7pjsdtnc",
                        "text": "H"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "mwylnv0o7qtcusk",
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "2"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "o5bjsyqcuqpljfn",
                        "text": "SO"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "1b23rij81t13xov",
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "4"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "n2y6u4kq8j6b6me",
                        "text": "     sulfuric acid"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1ix00hgw6tj4ibk",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "ueb5mw32yh85ihu",
                        "text": "Ba(OH)"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "l5oqf0xrrogfvv8",
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "2"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "9gnjmhz7c335a2x",
                        "text": "      barium hydroxide"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "uwqrk2ji06139ln",
                    "type": "td"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ulgo9vtidrwfwju",
                "type": "tr"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5ju0jtd6u9nlfzt",
            "rowstyle": "alternating",
            "type": "table"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Because strong acids and bases completely ionize, determining the [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] for acids or [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "] for bases is the same as calculating the concentration of cations or anions in aqueous solutions of soluble ionic compounds. (Note: H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " yields two H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions when it ionizes; only the first H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " fully ionizes. This is explained in more depth when we discuss polyprotic acids.)"
              }
            ],
            "id": "qfj5p1zthsdm639",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For example, because KNO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " is soluble in water, we know that every KNO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " formula unit dissociates into one K"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ion and one NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " ion and a 0.050-M KNO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " solution will therefore have [K"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] = [NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "] = 0.050 M. Likewise, in a 0.050-M solution of nitric acid, HNO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] = [NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "] = 0.050 M. Similarly, in a 0.050-M solution of the soluble base Ca(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", each Ca(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " dissociates into one Ca"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ion and two OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions, so [Ca"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] = 0.050 M and [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "] = 0.10 M. Knowing [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] or [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "] then allows us to determine pH."
              }
            ],
            "id": "gw9pse8tfxqsoyb",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "4049155551",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
